From 3dbdc510fc7c981b64f0feeadc52ff7a4e56d716 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:47:34 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: add WHNAT, the EN751221 PPE offload for WiFi
 without WED

The MT7603 and MT76x2 radios found next to the EN751221 have no WED, so
flows between WiFi and Ethernet are forwarded by the CPU. The vendor
driver still lets the PPE handle them (its "WHNAT"), with the CPU only
moving frames between mt76 and the frame engine:

- Frames received on a WiFi interface are tagged with the index of that
  interface and handed to the PPE through the LAN QDMA, channel 7. A
  bound flow leaves through its GDM port. An unbound one comes back to
  the CPU, where the PPE binds it, and is passed up on its interface.
- A flow towards a WiFi interface is bound with the CPU as destination
  and the interface index in its L2 tag. The PPE returns the rewritten
  frames with reason CRSN_22, and they are queued straight to the
  interface, batched per NAPI poll.

Do the same, behind CONFIG_NET_AIROHA_WHNAT (EN751221 only) and the
whnat_enable module parameter, which is off by default. mt76 registers
its interfaces with en75_whnat_register_vif() and offers each received
frame to en75_whnat_upstream_early() before GRO. It looks both up with
symbol_get(), so it does not depend on this driver.

Frames handed to the PPE have no netdev: they are not accounted by BQL
and their completion wakes no queue, so they may not take the last 32
LAN QDMA TX descriptors, which stay for the frames of the LAN netdevs.
The LAN QDMA RX buffers get 64 bytes of headroom, so that mt76 can push
its TX descriptor without reallocating. When an interface goes away its
flows are unbound and left out of binding until the flow table destroys
them. Only IPv4 flows are offloaded to WiFi.

Tested on a TP-Link Archer XR500v (EN7526G, MT7662 5 GHz, MT7603 2.4 GHz),
RAM-booted from airoha/openwrt with this change, both VPEs online. A phone
on 5 GHz VHT80 runs iperf3 (4 TCP streams) against a PC on the LAN, routed
and masqueraded by the router, with hardware flow offload on:

                         whnat_enable=0    whnat_enable=1
  download, 60 s         146.4 Mbit/s      411.3 Mbit/s
  bidirectional, 180 s   112.8 / 26.0      283.5 / 61.4 Mbit/s
  TCP retransmits/s      107.5             37.8
  CPU0 irq+softirq       88 %              46 %

1,912,332 frames were handed to the PPE and as many completed; no
warning in the log.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
